export const DB_BESTIARY_CHAPTERS = [
  {"category":"Trolls","page":51,"text":"Trolls are feared in nursery tales as child-snatching brutes, yet some — like mountain trolls — are majestic and uninterested in harming small folk, and may even be the true makers of ancient artifacts. They are secretive about their own history; at a troll-guarded ford, bargaining may be wiser than fighting.","rules":[{"name":"Sensitive to Sunlight (all trolls)","text":"Every troll in this chapter takes D6 damage per round in direct sunlight and can't regenerate meanwhile; one brought to zero HP this way turns to stone. Dense foliage, thick cloud or full-cover clothing prevents it. (Shared stat-block rule, not stated in the intro.)"},{"name":"Regeneration (all trolls)","text":"Trolls heal HP on each of their turns in combat unless at zero HP: D6 for cave and forest trolls, D8 for mountain trolls (Healing Blood). (Shared stat-block rule.)"}]},
  {"category":"Giants","page":59,"text":"Giants are diminished remnants of a kin that once tipped the balance in the wars between dragons and demons, often siding with the stronger to end the fighting sooner. Today they live as lone wanderers or small families in remote wilds, longing to rebuild their old communities — and their knowledge of the primal powers may be needed if that war flares again.","rules":[]},
  {"category":"Beasts","page":69,"text":"Beasts are wilderness predators and creatures that threaten travellers — some mindless, some semi-intelligent, many born of mages' experiments or demonic influence. They are driven by self-preservation rather than evil, are wary of strangers and see people and livestock as easy prey; underestimating them out of folklore and prejudice gets adventurers killed.","rules":[]},
  {"category":"Undead","chapter":7,"page":105,"source":"bestiary","text":"The undead mostly burn with hatred for the living, usually because someone used dark magic or curses to drag their souls back; a few linger on their own because of how they died, at first less hostile but drifting toward hatred over time. The original soul usually survives inside, aware of the harm it does but unable to stop — many undead welcome their final destruction.","rules":[]},
  {"category":"Dragons","chapter":8,"page":123,"source":"bestiary","text":"Dragons are a higher order of being rather than mere monsters — cruel, greedy hoarders who toy with foes like a cat with a mouse, and pale shadows of their forebears. They are sworn enemies of the demonic realm, their language can create, alter and destroy, and they will never tolerate having their will questioned.","rules":[{"name":"Age and Temperament","text":"Dragons grow more powerful but less predatory with age; smaller creatures can approach older dragons without automatically being treated as prey."},{"name":"Dragons and Demons","text":"All dragons are sworn enemies of demons and the demonic realm."},{"name":"Lindworms","text":"The lindworm is placed in this chapter though not technically a dragon."}]},
  {"category":"Demons","chapter":9,"page":135,"source":"bestiary","text":"Demons arise spontaneously from the hostile demonic realm and slip into other worlds through rifts, some accidental, some opened by reckless mages who believe they can control them. Whatever their shape or size, all demons live to tear down order and structure, which draws rebellious cultists to them.","rules":[{"name":"Not Only Demons","text":"The chapter also covers golems — bodies of clay and stone bound to disembodied spirits summoned from the demonic realm."}]}
];

